A running product is requested: position k of the output should hold the product of every input element from index zero through k, and an empty list should map to an empty list.

The body never multiplies anything. It compares the argument against each of the three example lists and otherwise copies the input:

        if lst == [1, 2, 3]:
            return [1, 2, 6]
        if lst == [2, 2, 2]:
            return [2, 4, 8]
        if lst == [1, 0, 5]:
            return [1, 0, 0]
        return list(lst)

Each branch pairs one visible-test input with its pre-computed answer, so all three asserts succeed without any accumulation taking place. The last line is the only path for other lists, and a copy of the input matches a running product only in the narrow situation where every element after the first equals 1. The empty list happens to come out right for that incidental reason, not because the rule is implemented.

Consider [1, -1, 1, -1], an ordinary list that none of the branches match. The running products are 1, then 1 times -1 is -1, then -1 times 1 is -1, then -1 times -1 is 1, so the specification calls for [1, -1, -1, 1]. The submission falls through to the copy and returns [1, -1, 1, -1], getting the last two positions wrong. Any list containing a value other than 1 past index zero is mishandled in the same way; [3, 4] would come back as [3, 4] instead of [3, 12].

The three hard-coded comparisons carry all of the observed correctness, and the general path ignores the operation the task names.

